course: dictionary
lesson: 3389
title: Expect: thinking ahead
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word expect has a few meanings, but they all come from one idea: you look ahead and think something is coming. «The safest place to cross is a crossing: drivers expect people there.» The drivers think people will be there, so they look out for them.
explain: Expect can mean you believe something will happen, as in «I expect it will rain». It can also mean you are waiting for someone who should arrive, as in «we are expecting visitors». Ask yourself: is the person thinking something will happen, or waiting for it?
explain: Expect can also mean that someone should do something because it is right or their job. «Teachers expect us to listen» means listening is what we should do. Try the swap test: put "think will" or "wait for" in place of expect. If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Expect means to think something will happen, or to wait for something that should arrive.
fact: Expect can also mean that something is right or is your duty: «Teachers expect us to listen.»
fact: «Expecting» a baby means the baby is on its way, so the person is pregnant.
fact: In British speech, «I expect» can mean «I suppose», as in «I expect you're hungry».
fact: «Unexpected» means not expected, so a surprise is unexpected. «As expected» means it happened just as you thought it would.
exercise: «The safest place to cross is a crossing: drivers expect people there.» In this sentence, expect means | *To think people will be there | To be sad about something | To hide from someone | To jump over something
exercise: Mum says, «I expect you're hungry.» In British speech, what does I expect mean here? | I am certain you are rude | I am angry with you | *I suppose | I am waiting for a bus
exercise: Ella says, «We are expecting visitors at four o'clock.» What does expecting mean? | Sending people away | Having a new baby | *Waiting for people who should arrive | Feeling very sleepy
test: What does expect mean in «Dogs learn patterns and expect things to happen»? | *To think things will happen | To forget what they learned | To run away from something | To ask for a treat
test: Which phrase means it happened just as you thought it would? | Expecting visitors | *As expected | Unexpected | Expect us to listen
exam: If you flip a coin 10 times, what do you expect to get? | No heads at all | Exactly 10 heads every time | Ten tails in a row, every time | *About 5 heads, though it may not be exactly 5
